 :the-result-the-ranking-could-not-produce
 {:finding "Neither top hypothesis is the answer. The pair is, and it is worth
            far more than their sum."
  :why "The served hydrate has two halves. A (514 ms, 84 gets) is hot-datoms:
        fetching, decoding and merging blocks. B (1,430 ms) is building the
        LocalStore map, parsing every value twice. h1 removes A and leaves B.
        h7 removes B and leaves A. Each alone is floored by the other's
        remainder."
  :h1-alone 29.26 :h7-alone 40.92 :pair 0.76
  :order "h7 first -- it is reversible, :M rather than :L, captures the larger
          CPU half on its own, and unlike h1 does not depend on a read:write
          ratio nothing has measured."
  :note "A tournament scores one change at a time against one baseline, so it
         is structurally unable to see this. Evolve is where it was recovered."}

 :what-the-loop-corrected-about-its-own-first-run
 [{:what "h1 credited with 98.4% of the cost"
   :now "58.2%"
   :why "The first run credited a seed MV with the whole hydrate. The
         decomposition landed later that day showed it removes A only -- 23% of
         the CPU. B survives ANY encoding, because the consumer is a
         synchronous LocalStore over a plain Clojure map."}
  {:what "h2 (fold) credited with 57.5%"
   :now "39.2%"
   :why "The first run used an offline harness row that measured a different
         quantity. Fold reduces the novelty part of A, not B."}
  {:what "h4 (CARv2 pack) credited with 38.6% and ranked third"
   :now "10.3%"
   :why "An arithmetic error in the input: it assumed novelty packs and
         snapshot does not, when write-locality makes each novelty cell its own
         commit and therefore its own pack. Only the 21 snapshot blocks
         coalesce. The judge made this visible by producing a result that
         contradicted the ADR the hypothesis cited."}
  {:what "the baseline itself"
   :now "$57.82 -> $70.06, and the Aura crossover 1.12M -> 0.93M queries/month"
   :why "The corrected measurement made kotobase look WORSE, not better. This
         is recorded because a loop that only ever improves its subject is
         measuring its author's preferences."}]

 :what-the-judge-cannot-see
 ["CPU. The dominant input is a Node proxy for a quantity only the platform can
   read. Paired `wrangler tail` showed one request reporting 36 ms of
   in-isolate elapsed spending 4,046 ms of cpuTime (n=1). Nothing inside the
   isolate can see cpuTime, so this cannot be closed from here, and every
   dollar figure inherits the uncertainty. Do not quote these as prices."
  "The read:write ratio, which decides whether h1 is a win at all."
  "The read side at all: a five-minute tail of the whole Worker returned no
   served queries. Today's value is the unit-economics crossover, not saved CPU
   on traffic that is not there."
  "Memory. A 4.9 MB seed decode against a 128 MB isolate is unmeasured, and a
   hypothesis that fails on memory scores the same here as one that succeeds."
  "Correctness. Every hypothesis is assumed to return the same answers; the
   judge only prices them."
  "Storage, deliberately excluded as constant across hypotheses -- including it
   would shrink every difference by a constant and flatter whichever change is
   being argued for."]

 :seed-for-next
 "Close the CPU uncertainty first; it is the input every ranking rests on and
  the only one that cannot be read from inside the isolate. A `wrangler tail`
  harness that pairs cpuTime against the counters /_diag/hydrate already
  reports would make the judge's dominant term measured rather than proxied.
  Until then this loop ranks reliably and prices unreliably."}
